Extend $AM$ to $D$ so that $MD = MA$.  Then triangles $AMB$ and $DMC$ are congruent, so triangles $ABC$ and $ACD$ have equal area.

[asy]
unitsize(0.3 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, M;

A = (-7/sqrt(37),42/sqrt(37));
B = (0,0);
C = (2*sqrt(37),0);
M = (B + C)/2;
D = 2*M - A;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--D--C);

label("$A$", A, dir(90));
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, NE);
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$M$", M, SW);

label("$7$", (A + B)/2, W);
label("$15$", (A + C)/2, NE);
label("$10$", (A + M)/2, SW);
label("$10$", (D + M)/2, SW);
label("$7$", (C + D)/2, E);
[/asy]

